# Build output
**/bin/
**/obj/
dist/
node_modules/
# Any go build output in the tool folder. SigilEdit.exe is what build.ps1 asks
# for; a bare `go build .` names the binary after the module (sigiledittool.exe),
# and that stray copy is not a second tool.
SigilEditTool/*.exe
# The ~ copy a build can leave next to the binary (Go/Wails writes one when it
# replaces a running exe). Glob above does not cover it, and it is 13MB of nothing.
SigilEditTool/*.exe~
*.zip

# Editors / tooling
.idea/
.codegraph/
.tmp-*/
*.pdb

# Development-only inputs.
#
# The game's own tables, the toolchain that reads them, and the databases the
# asset scripts build from. The assets those scripts produce
# (SigilEditTool/assets/skillnames.*.json and skillinfo.json) are committed, so
# neither a build nor a release needs any of this - only regenerating them does,
# and that requires a copy of the game.
GBFRDataTools/
extracted/
*.db
sqlite3.exe
msg_*.json

# The service bindings are generated (`wails3 generate bindings`) and nothing in
# src imports them: the frontend talks to the backend by name, through
# Call.ByName and Events.On. Generated code that nothing reads does not belong in
# a diff, and ignoring it is what keeps a wails upgrade from carrying a
# "regenerate bindings" commit.
#
# What stays tracked is the two files under
# frontend/bindings/github.com/wailsapp/wails/v3/internal/: the vite wails plugin
# imports eventcreate at build time and fails the build without it, so those two
# have to be in the tree for `npm run build` to work with no wails CLI installed.
SigilEditTool/frontend/bindings/sigiledittool/

# Session tooling: the agent harness drops a run-continuation marker here.
# Not part of the project, and it is recreated whenever it is deleted.
.omo/
